task: mail-loopback-tls
agent: opus5-intent
started: 2026-09-05
branch: feature/mail-loopback-tls
worktree: ../okay-wt-mtls

why: I shipped okay-mail an hour ago with its STARTTLS path covered by
  the PURE tests and never exercised over a real socket, and said so.
  That is worse than it sounds: the pure tests prove the client
  DECIDES to upgrade; the code that performs the upgrade — Tls.client
  over the live socket, the new reader and writer, the second EHLO —
  has never once run. An unverified upgrade in a mail client is the
  path where credentials would travel in the clear if it silently did
  not happen.

what is actually missing, and it is not in okay-mail: okay-tls has
  `client(sock, ...)` to upgrade a connected CLIENT socket and no
  mirror for the server side. STARTTLS is a two-sided upgrade — SMTP,
  IMAP, XMPP and Postgres all do it — and the seam has only one side,
  which is why my test could not be written.

  So: `Tls.server(sock, cert, key, secrets)`, the mirror of
  `Tls.client`, and then the loopback SMTP server upgrades with it and
  the client's whole STARTTLS path runs for real.

not my module: okay-tls belongs to whoever wrote it. The addition is
  symmetric to what is there, not a change to it, and I will say so in
  the room.
